FINDINGS:
1. [BLOCKER] src/vm.rs:1126 — `Statement::Try` returns `Err(failure)` before running `finally` when there is no `catch`, so `try / set bad to 1 + "one" / finally / say "cleanup" / end` never runs the cleanup on the tree VM while the bytecode VM runs it (and SPEC.md:803-813 shows `try/finally` with no `catch` as valid) — two VMs disagree and a promised cleanup is skipped — concrete fix: run `self.run_finally_body(finally_body)?` before `return Err(failure)` and propagate afterwards.
2. [BLOCKER] src/bytecode/vm.rs:2555 — `handle_failure` returns `Ok(true)` even when the handler has no `catch`, so `try / set bad to 1 + "one" / end` and `try / set bad to 1 + "one" / finally / say "cleanup" / end` swallow the failure and resume past the `NOP` where the tree VM propagates it — a caught-vs-propagated disagreement that the corpus misses because `try/only-a-finally` only exercises the success path — concrete fix: when `handler.catch.is_none()`, run the `finally` if present then return `Ok(false)` so the original error propagates.
3. [BLOCKER] src/bytecode/vm.rs:1920 — `turn_over_to` (`SKIP`) jumps a `while` to its `top` without calling `charge_loop`, while the tree VM charges every turn up front at src/vm.rs:1062, so `while` with `skip` on every turn never spends its per-loop iteration cap on the bytecode VM (only the 10M step budget stops it) where the tree VM raises `Maximum of N iterations` — uncharged iteration plus VM disagreement, missed because `edge_a_skip_is_charged_as_one_iteration` only covers `repeat` — concrete fix: create/look up the owner entry and call `charge_loop` before `set_ip` in `turn_over_to` for the non-iterator case, mirroring `jump`.
REVIEW VERDICT: FINDINGS 3
